    id: 'cs042', t: 'cross',
    p: 'Text 1\nWhy did some human groups begin cultivating plants roughly 11,000 years ago? Archaeologist Marcus Feld argues that farming arose out of necessity. As populations of hunter-gatherers grew, he contends, wild food became scarcer relative to the number of people, and groups turned to the laborious work of cultivation only because foraging could no longer sustain them.\n\nText 2\nExcavations led by archaeologist Leila Nasser at several of the earliest known cultivation sites in Southwest Asia have recovered abundant remains of wild game, fish, nuts, and wild grasses from the same layers that contain the first cultivated cereals. Nasser notes that these communities appear to have been well fed, and she suggests that early cultivation may have begun as a supplement—for example, to produce grain for feasts—rather than as a response to hunger.',
    q: 'Based on the texts, how would Nasser (Text 2) most likely respond to Feld\'s argument in Text 1?',
    o: [
      'By pointing to evidence that cultivation began in places where wild food was still plentiful',
      'By agreeing that population growth forced early communities to abandon foraging entirely',
      'By arguing that early cultivated cereals were less nutritious than the wild foods they replaced',
      'By arguing that hunter-gatherer populations had stopped growing before cultivation began',
    ],
    a: 0,
    tr: [null, 'opposite', 'offscope', 'unsupported'],
